1. Pull the cert from your vCenter host
bash
python3 get_cert.py <vcenter-host-ip-or-hostname> /opt/vcenter-watcher/vcenter.pem

This prints the subject and issuer so you can immediately see whether it's self-signed (subject == issuer) or CA-signed (subject != issuer).

2. If subject != issuer (CA-signed, typical for vCenter with VMCA)

Download the full chain from vCenter's built-in endpoint:

bash
curl -k -o /tmp/download.zip "https://<vcenter-host>/certs/download.zip"
cd /tmp && unzip download.zip
find /tmp -iname "*.crt" -o -iname "*.0"
cat /tmp/certs/lin/*.0 > /opt/vcenter-watcher/vcenter-chain.pem

Then verify the chain actually validates:

bash
openssl verify -CAfile /opt/vcenter-watcher/vcenter-chain.pem /opt/vcenter-watcher/vcenter.pem

Should print OK. Update CA_CERT_PATH in .env to point at vcenter-chain.pem.

3. If subject == issuer (self-signed, typical for bare ESXi)

The single cert from step 1 is the complete trust anchor — no chain needed. Just point CA_CERT_PATH at vcenter.pem directly.

4. Test actual API communication
bash
/opt/vcenter-watcher/venv/bin/python3 -c "
from pyVim.connect import SmartConnect
import ssl
ctx = ssl.create_default_context(cafile='/opt/vcenter-watcher/vcenter-chain.pem')
si = SmartConnect(host='<host>', user='<user>', pwd='<password>', sslContext=ctx)
print('apiType:', si.content.about.apiType)
print('fullName:', si.content.about.fullName)
"
apiType: VirtualCenter → talking to vCenter
apiType: HostAgent → talking directly to ESXi
5. Restart the service once confirmed
bash
sudo systemctl restart vcenter-watcher
sudo journalctl -u vcenter-watcher -f
